  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periodic showers and thunderstorms expected \nthrough this evening. Otherwise, quiet weather is likely \nthrough Labor Day.\n\nNW'erly flow aloft and embedded shortwave troughs impact Western\nand Central NY through tonight. At the surface, a high pressure\nridge builds slowly from northern ON and vicinity. Periods of\nisolated to scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are \nexpected through this evening, roughly along and southwest of a \nYoungstown to Dansville line in our CWA, as primarily weak \nboundary layer instability is released by the following: low- \nlevel convergence and moist ascent along surface trough axes \naccompanying the shortwave troughs; downshear outflow boundaries\ninteracting favorably with low-level vertical wind shear \nassociated with moderate to strong deep-layer bulk shear. The\ngreatest potential for a few thunderstorms exists in the \nWestern Southern Tier, where boundary layer CAPE is expected to \nbe greater. Despite the sizable deep-layer bulk shear \nmagnitudes, severe thunderstorms are not expected due to \nrelatively-limited instability. Showers and any storms are \nexpected to end by midnight tonight as the boundary layer \nstabilizes via nocturnal cooling. After highs in the 70's to \nnear 80F late this afternoon, lows should reach mainly the upper\n40's to upper 50's around daybreak Saturday courtesy of partial\nclearing, weak or calm surface winds, and a cooler/drier air \nmass associated with the building surface ridge. The partial \nclearing, weak or calm surface winds, and lingering low-level \nmoisture from recent rainfall should yield patchy steam and \nradiation fog along and near the river and lake valleys east of \nLake Ontario, in the Finger Lakes, and in the Western Southern \nTier around daybreak.\n\nCurrent odds continue to favor fair weather this Saturday \nthrough Labor Day as the surface ridge settles S'ward and over \nour region, and the ridge aloft builds from the north-central\nUnited States. However, isolated rain showers may impact the \nEastern Lake Ontario Region at times on Saturday and Sunday as \nshortwave troughs embedded in the NW'erly flow aloft impact that\npart of our CWA. Near-average to slightly below-average daily \nhigh and low temperatures are expected through Labor Day as the \naforementioned ridge is accompanied by a cooler and drier air \nmass. Mainly clear sky, weak or calm surface winds, and \nsufficient low-level moisture should yield patchy steam and \nradiation fog in/near the river and lake valleys east of Lake \nOntario, in the Finger Lakes, and in the Western Southern Tier \naround daybreak on Sunday and Labor Day, respectively.",
